WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.738
The third bypass sits on the access layer.

2
00:00:02.738 --> 00:00:07.704
A rogue DHCP server handing out a
malicious resolver can move a whole

3
00:00:07.704 --> 00:00:12.149
subnet onto an attacker's DNS in
one step, past every block list.

4
00:00:12.149 --> 00:00:16.475
In IPv6, rogue router advertisements
do the same, and they're often

5
00:00:16.475 --> 00:00:20.800
the bigger surface, because many
networks don't govern them at all.

6
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:26.238
Other DHCP options steer traffic too, like
the search list and the proxy setting.

7
00:00:26.238 --> 00:00:29.201
The Windows safeguard is
narrower than it looks:

8
00:00:29.201 --> 00:00:34.238
it stops a misconfigured Windows server,
not a home router plugged into a wall port.

9
00:00:34.238 --> 00:00:38.007
That takes DHCP snooping and
RA Guard on the switches,

10
00:00:38.007 --> 00:00:43.662
central option management, and IPAM so
an unexpected lease is visible at once.

11
00:00:43.662 --> 00:00:46.128
And since prevention is never complete,

12
00:00:46.128 --> 00:00:49.920
watch for hosts whose queries
never reach the governed logs.
